{
  "image_name": "3aa2151a4361ada009aace834258b791.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how prebiotics benefit gut and whole-body health through effects on intestinal microbes, epithelial cells, and immunity. In the gut lumen, prebiotics are selectively fermented by beneficial bacteria, increasing fecal bulk and water binding, which leads to softer stools, shorter transit time, stronger peristaltic reflexes, and overall improved bowel habits.\n\nFermentation also generates short-chain fatty acids, especially acetate, butyrate, and propionate. These metabolites lower intestinal pH, which appears to suppress pathogens by creating antimicrobial conditions and increasing competition for nutrients. The same drop in pH is linked to improved mineral absorption, illustrated with calcium, likely through greater solubility.\n\nThe figure also highlights immune effects at the intestinal barrier. Prebiotics seem to interact with dendritic cells and T cells, promoting immunomodulation and reduced allergy. Through M cells and immune stimulation, they are associated with increased natural killer cell and phagocytic activity, strengthening defense against pathogens and reducing infections.\n\nAt the epithelial level, prebiotics and their metabolites reinforce barrier function by increasing markers such as ZO-1 and occludin while lowering zonulin. This supports tight-junction integrity, reduces LPS translocation, and lowers inflammation. Additional hormonal effects, including increased GLP-1, PYY, and leptin with reduced ghrelin, are linked to greater satiety.",
